 "section_summary": "This section contains documents concerning the administration, military infrastructure, and territorial status of Okinawa and outlying Japanese islands under post-World War II U.S. military occupation. It encompasses background orientation materials for occupation troops on Okinawa's economy and battle sites, a July 1947 U.S. Army Corps of Engineers Information Bulletin detailing regional construction programs, and Military Government Summation No. 10 covering political, economic, and public health activities across the Ryukyus in August 1947. Additionally, it preserves a diplomatic despatch from the U.S. Political Adviser for Japan transmitting Part IV of a Japanese Foreign Office study documenting the history, administration, and industries of minor islands in the Pacific and Sea of Japan, particularly the Daito Island Group.",
 "subfiles": [
  {
   "about": "Orientation handbook for U.S. military personnel detailing the geography, transportation, agriculture, domestic industries, and historical battlefields and sightseeing itineraries of Okinawa.",
   "continues_previous": true,
   "date": "",
   "doc_type": "handbook",
   "end_page": 222,
   "from": "Special Service Office, Okinawa Base Command",
   "keywords": [
    "Okinawa",
    "agriculture",
    "sugar industry",
    "battlefields",
    "Shuri",
    "OBASCOM",
    "tourism"
   ],
   "start_page": 210,
   "summary": "This guide provides background information on Okinawa's civilian economy, highlighting agriculture, sweet potato and sugar cane production, local fisheries, and traditional handicrafts. It outlines planned tours sponsored by OBASCOM Special Service to historic castles, shrines, and major combat sites from the 1945 campaign, including Hacksaw Ridge, Sugar Loaf Hill, and Shuri Castle. The text describes wartime devastation and initial recovery efforts while serving as a cultural orientation for American occupation forces.",
   "title": "Okinawa Background Information",
   "to": "U.S. Armed Forces personnel"
  },
  {
   "about": "Information bulletin outlining the organization, civilian contracting arrangements, and military construction program of the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers Okinawa District.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1947-07-21",
   "doc_type": "bulletin",
   "end_page": 231,
   "from": "Office of the District Engineer, Okinawa District, Corps of Engineers, War Department",
   "keywords": [
    "Corps of Engineers",
    "Okinawa Engineer District",
    "military construction",
    "Camp Kue",
    "Camp Futema",
    "contractors",
    "infrastructure"
   ],
   "start_page": 223,
   "summary": "This bulletin describes the mission, structure, and operational transition of the Okinawa Engineer District under the Western Ocean Division. It explains the shift from military troop labor to civilian cost-plus-a-fixed-fee (CPFF) contractors, primarily Holmes & Narver and Guy F. Atkinson Company with J.A. Jones Construction Company. The bulletin details interim and typhoon-proof construction programs totaling thirty-one million dollars, including military housing, airfields, port facilities, and major cantonments at Camp Kue and Camp Futema.",
   "title": "Information Bulletin: Mission, Structure and Program of the Okinawa District",
   "to": ""
  },
  {
   "about": "Monthly military government report summarizing political, legal, economic, and social developments across the Ryukyu Islands during August 1947.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1947-08",
   "doc_type": "report",
   "end_page": 273,
   "from": "General Headquarters, Commander-in-Chief, Far East",
   "keywords": [
    "Military Government",
    "Ryukyu Islands",
    "public safety",
    "food rationing",
    "fisheries",
    "Japanese B encephalitis",
    "resettlement",
    "finance"
   ],
   "start_page": 232,
   "summary": "This comprehensive report reviews U.S. Military Government operations, covering directives, party politics, civilian courts, and police anti-smuggling campaigns. The economic section reports on crop yields, fishing output, forestry on Iriomote, phosphate operations on Kita Daito, and currency circulation across the northern, central, and southern Ryukyus. The social affairs section surveys welfare rolls, housing reconstruction, educational reforms, and medical matters, including an outbreak of Japanese B encephalitis and extensive DDT sanitation programs.",
   "title": "Summation No. 10: United States Army Military Government Activities in the Ryukyus for the Month of August 1947",
   "to": ""
  },
  {
   "about": "Diplomatic despatch forwarding twenty copies of Part IV of the Japanese Foreign Office study on minor islands adjacent to Japan proper.",
   "continues_previous": false,
   "date": "1947-10-22",
   "doc_type": "despatch",
   "end_page": 274,
